WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.567
Congestion, Lesson 4: RUDY

2
00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:07.356
Before routing, estimate where it will be crowded.

3
00:00:07.956 --> 00:00:13.522
A 12 × 8 chip, six pins, six nets.

4
00:00:13.522 --> 00:00:17.533
Dashed lines show connections, not routes.

5
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:22.211
The six nets' HPWL adds up to 34.

6
00:00:22.211 --> 00:00:26.667
But the total cannot say where wires will crowd.

7
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:32.411
Cut the chip into 4 × 2 routing tiles, called GCells.

8
00:00:32.411 --> 00:00:35.089
Each tile is 3 wide and 4 tall.

9
00:00:35.089 --> 00:00:41.100
Demand is stored as demand[x][y]: column first.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.000
What a tile can hold is its capacity; here 2.

11
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.967
Start with one net: A–B.

12
00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:54.867
Its box is 7 wide and 0 tall, so HPWL is 7.

13
00:00:54.867 --> 00:01:00.656
Left end x=1 is in column 0; right end x=8 is in column 2.

14
00:01:00.656 --> 00:01:05.111
Every tile between them is selected: 3 tiles.

15
00:01:05.111 --> 00:01:09.122
Each tile gets 7 ÷ 3, about 2.33.

16
00:01:09.722 --> 00:01:12.844
Add 7/3 to each of the three tiles.

17
00:01:12.844 --> 00:01:17.300
Against capacity 2, each is already 1/3 over.

18
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.422
That is overflow: max(0, demand − capacity).

19
00:01:23.222 --> 00:01:28.344
A boundary detail: watch a pin land exactly on x=3.

20
00:01:28.344 --> 00:01:33.689
floor(3 ÷ 3) = 1, so it counts in the next column.

21
00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:39.033
(1,1) to (3,1): two tiles, 1 each.

22
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:43.933
Coincident pins: HPWL is 0, so add 0.

23
00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:50.522
Net 2, C–D: HPWL 7, 3 tiles, 7/3 each.

24
00:01:50.522 --> 00:01:55.867
Net 3, A–C: HPWL 4, 2 tiles, 2 each.

25
00:01:55.867 --> 00:02:01.211
Net 4, B–D: HPWL 4, 2 tiles, 2 each.

26
00:02:01.211 --> 00:02:08.333
Net 5, A–B–C–D: HPWL 11, 6 tiles, 11/6 each.

27
00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:14.122
Net 6, E–F: HPWL 1, 2 tiles, 1/2 each.

28
00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:19.022
Note: this is a partial sum, not the final demand.

29
00:02:19.622 --> 00:02:24.078
All six added: now this is the full demand map.

30
00:02:24.078 --> 00:02:29.422
The eight tiles sum to 34, exactly the total HPWL.

31
00:02:29.422 --> 00:02:33.433
In this model, demand is conserved.

32
00:02:34.233 --> 00:02:38.467
Capacity 2: any tile with ratio above 1 overflows.

33
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:43.367
Six tiles overflow; total 22, max 14/3.

34
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:47.822
Set capacity to 1: not one demand number changes.

35
00:02:47.822 --> 00:02:52.278
Only ratio and overflow change: total overflow 28.

36
00:02:53.078 --> 00:02:57.756
Now the teacher's other placement: a centre cluster.

37
00:02:57.756 --> 00:03:01.100
All demand lands in one tile: 7.

38
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.778
Both maps share one legend: same colour, same value.

39
00:03:05.778 --> 00:03:10.678
Spread out, max overflow drops from 5 to 4.67.

40
00:03:10.678 --> 00:03:14.022
But total overflow rises from 5 to 22.

41
00:03:14.022 --> 00:03:18.922
Here total demand is total wirelength: 7 vs 34.

42
00:03:18.922 --> 00:03:23.378
Before saying 'better', name the metric.

43
00:03:24.378 --> 00:03:29.056
Same pins, now shared by overlap area.

44
00:03:29.056 --> 00:03:35.956
DREAMPlace and OpenROAD code both work this way.

45
00:03:35.956 --> 00:03:40.411
Tiles in the middle of a box get more, corners less.

46
00:03:40.411 --> 00:03:45.533
Computed this way, the spread's max overflow is 5.54.

47
00:03:45.533 --> 00:03:50.656
Conclusions move with the model: say which RUDY.

48
00:03:51.656 --> 00:03:57.000
RUDY is a fast pre-routing estimate, not a router.

49
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.456
Real capacity is split by direction and by metal layer.

50
00:04:01.456 --> 00:04:07.244
Multi-pin nets run longer; RISA scales them up.

51
00:04:07.244 --> 00:04:11.256
Its best use is quick feedback during placement.

52
00:04:12.056 --> 00:04:16.067
Pause the film and press 'Try this frame'

53
00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:20.744
to take these pins and this grid into the lab.
